## Common Pitfalls & Solutions

### 1. Recycling Behavior
```r
# R recycles shorter vector
c(1, 2, 3) + c(10, 20)  # c(11, 22, 13) ⚠️ Unexpected!

# Use explicit recycling
rep(c(10, 20), length.out = 3)
```

### 2. Factor Confusion
```r
# Factors look like strings but are numeric underneath
x <- factor(c("a", "b", "a"))
x + 1  # Error! (cannot add numeric to factor)

# Convert to string first
as.character(x) + 1  # Still error! (numeric needed)
```

### 3. Data Frame Column Access
```r
# [[]] vs []
df[["col"]]  # Returns vector
df["col"]    # Returns data frame
df$col       # Returns vector

# Be aware when writing functions
subset_col <- function(data, col) {
  return(data[[col]])  # Use [[ to get vector
}
```

### 4. == vs identical()
```r
c(1, 2, 3) == c(1, 2, 3)  # c(TRUE, TRUE, TRUE)
c(1, 2, 3) == c(1, 2, 3)  # Compare element-wise

# Use identical() only when comparing single objects
identical(1, 1.0)  # FALSE (different types internally)

# For vectors use all.equal()
all.equal(1.0, 1.0000001)  # TRUE (numeric tolerance)
```

### 5. Modifying in Place
```r
# Modifying doesn't always modify in place
df$new_col <- df$old_col * 2  # Safe
df[df$age > 30, "name"] <- "Adult"  # Safe

# Be careful with functions
f <- function(x) {
  x[1] <- 999
  x
}
y <- c(1, 2, 3)
f(y)  # Returns c(999, 2, 3)
y     # Still c(1, 2, 3) - not modified!
```

