### Q10: How do you handle missing data (NA values) in R?

**A:**
```r
# Creating NA values
x <- c(1, NA, 3, NA, 5)

# Detecting NA
is.na(x)  # c(FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE)
sum(is.na(x))  # 2 NA values

# Removing NA
na.omit(x)      # c(1, 3, 5)
x[!is.na(x)]    # c(1, 3, 5)

# Operating with NA
mean(x)  # NA (propagates!)
mean(x, na.rm = TRUE)  # 2.75 (ignores NA)

# Types of missing values
NA       # Generic missing
NULL     # "no value" (different from NA)
NaN      # "Not a Number" (0/0)
Inf      # Infinity

# Checking for these
is.na(NA)   # TRUE
is.null(NULL)  # TRUE
is.nan(NaN)  # TRUE
is.finite(Inf)  # FALSE

# In data frames
df <- data.frame(x = c(1, NA, 3), y = c(NA, 2, 3))

# Remove rows with ANY NA
na.omit(df)

# Remove rows with NA in specific column
df[!is.na(df$x), ]

# Replace NA
df$x[is.na(df$x)] <- 0  # Replace NA with 0
```

**Best practices:**
1. Always check for NA with `sum(is.na(x))`
2. Most functions have `na.rm` parameter
3. Use `na.omit()` carefully - removes entire rows
4. Document why data is missing if possible

