### Q14: What's the difference between T-test and ANOVA?

**A:**
```r
# T-TEST: Compare 2 groups
x <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
y <- c(2, 3, 4, 5, 6)

t.test(x, y)
# Null hypothesis: x and y have same mean
# Returns: t-statistic, p-value, confidence interval

# One-sample t-test (vs known value)
t.test(x, mu = 3)

# Paired t-test (same subjects, before/after)
before <- c(100, 110, 120)
after <- c(105, 115, 122)
t.test(before, after, paired = TRUE)

# ANOVA: Compare 3+ groups
groups <- factor(rep(c("A", "B", "C"), each = 10))
data <- c(rnorm(10, mean = 0),
          rnorm(10, mean = 1),
          rnorm(10, mean = 2))

aov(data ~ groups)
# Null hypothesis: all groups have same mean

# Chi-squared test: Categorical associations
table <- matrix(c(10, 20, 30, 40), nrow = 2)
chisq.test(table)
```

**When to use each:**
- **T-test**: 2 groups, continuous data, normal distribution
- **ANOVA**: 3+ groups, continuous data, normal distribution
- **Chi-squared**: Categorical data, frequencies
- **Kruskal-Wallis**: Non-normal data (non-parametric ANOVA)

