### Data Types
**Files**: R_COMPLETE_NOTES.md (lines ~50-400)

#### 1. Numeric (Double & Integer)
```r
x <- 5.5    # Double (decimal)
y <- 5L     # Integer (explicit)
typeof(x)   # "double"
```
- **Use When**: Measurements, counts, calculations
- **Key Distinction**: 5 vs 5L (same value, different type)
- **Operations**: +, -, *, /, ^, %%, %/%

**Theory**: Doubles use 64 bits (precise), integers use less memory

#### 2. Character (Strings)
```r
name <- "John"
paste("Hello", name)    # "Hello John"
substr("Hello", 1, 3)   # "Hel" (positions 1-3)
```
- **Use When**: Text, names, labels
- **Key Functions**: paste(), nchar(), substr(), toupper(), tolower()
- **Important**: Positions start at 1 (not 0)

**Theory**: Immutable text - can't modify in place

#### 3. Logical (Boolean)
```r
TRUE & FALSE    # AND
TRUE | FALSE    # OR
!TRUE           # NOT
5 > 3           # TRUE
```
- **Use When**: Conditions, filtering, decisions
- **Key Distinction**: & (vectorized) vs && (single value)
- **Operations**: &, |, !, ==, !=, >, <, >=, <=

**Theory**: Coerces to 1 (TRUE) and 0 (FALSE) in math

#### 4. Special Values
```r
NULL        # Nothing
NA          # Missing
NaN         # Invalid
Inf         # Infinity
```
- **Check With**: is.na(), is.null(), is.nan(), is.infinite()
- **Propagation**: NA in calculations produces NA
- **Handling**: Use na.rm=TRUE in functions

**Theory**: NA means "unknown", NaN means "impossible calculation"

#### 5. Type Coercion
```r
c(1, "2")           # c("1", "2") - becomes character
as.numeric("42")    # 42
as.character(42)    # "42"
```
- **Hierarchy**: logical < integer < double < character
- **Why**: Preserves information (character can represent anything)
- **Common Issue**: Invisible coercion causes bugs

**Theory**: R automatically converts to highest type in hierarchy

**KEYPOINTS: Data Types**
- Use `typeof()` for exact type, `class()` for general class
- Hierarchy: logical < integer < double < character
- NA propagates through calculations
- 1-based indexing throughout R

